WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:06.900
Primary Care Support Services
Barbara Hakin

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:10.680
Chief Operating Officer
Overview of the paper

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.310
Thank you, Chairman.

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:16.480
This paper is to do two or three things.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:22.110
Firstly, to keep the Board up to date about
this programme, which I would say from the

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:30.329
outset is an absolutely massive programme
of change that we need to deliver safely,

00:00:30.329 --> 00:00:34.240
efficiently and effectively over the next
12 months.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:42.930
This is a service with 1,800 staff, worth
£100 million, and the changes are very significant

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:43.930
indeed.

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:48.190
It is an update on where we are in that transformational
process.

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:53.940
I hope that it give the Board reassurance
that we are managing and mitigating the risks,

00:00:53.940 --> 00:01:00.010
although they still exist quite significantly,
and I think the risks for NHS England getting

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:05.370
this process right are still significant.

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:10.490
Most importantly, perhaps, it asks you to
launch a consultation with staff, which we

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:12.539
now feel ready to initiate.

00:01:12.539 --> 00:01:16.409
History of the programme
If I can remind everyone about primary care

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:21.799
support services and the history behind why
we are discussing this today, the table in

00:01:21.799 --> 00:01:23.700
1 identifies exactly what they are.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:34.840
They are dominantly about paying independent
contractors, particularly GPs, optometrists

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:41.330
and pharmacists, but also in supporting them
to do some of their processes.

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:44.490
Where it says cervical screening and breast
screening, it does not actually mean that

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:48.529
these services undertake cervical screening
and breast screening, but they do the call

00:01:48.529 --> 00:01:51.340
and recall system to make sure that no-one
is missed.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:56.060
These are a key set of essential services
that, in the NHS over the years, have been

00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:00.810
dealt with in very different ways in very
many different units.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:05.149
Once upon a time, I think the highest number
of commissioning units we had was probably

00:02:05.149 --> 00:02:11.530
when we had over 300 PCTs, and some of these
are still in a legacy, so we had 300 different

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:16.280
ways of providing and supporting these services.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:21.790
We decided that because these were so critical
both to the payment of the contractors and

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:27.620
to delivering services for staff, we would
actually simply what we call lift and shift

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:30.030
the services in the first year.

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:34.900
There was so much change going on in NHS England
in terms of our other reorganisation that

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:40.390
it was deemed prudent not to reorganise these
services at the same time, and we were able

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:50.890
to do that because we were in the final year
of the running cost reduction that we were

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:51.890
obliged to make.

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:57.980
Therefore, we were able to use that slack
capacity in order to leave these services

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:00.860
at their full running costs for an extra year.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:04.590
Nonetheless, there was no question when we
made the decision to defer that we were still

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:09.730
committed to these services being subject
to the same running cost reduction that all

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:14.450
the services in NHS England had been.

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:20.750
We discussed this informally with the Board
some months ago when our plan was to ensure

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:27.480
that, in line with that timetable, we had
completely transformed these services by April

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.440
2014.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:34.410
After discussion, it was clear that this was
too high risk for these very important services,

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:40.630
and the Board advised that we should revisit
the terms of reference, which were initially

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:47.110
largely to reduce running costs, to say, 'How
can we ensure that these services are transformed

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:52.870
safely, and what is the timetable to do that,
still needing to get to the point whereby

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:57.110
we do make these management cost reductions,
and actually, might we even at some stage

00:03:57.110 --> 00:03:58.410
be able to go beyond that?

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:04.160
Might there be more economies of scale because
of changes in technology and the digital revolution?'

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:09.250
We have re-looked at things, had many pieces
of work going on, and what we are bringing

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:15.580
to you today is some outline proposals that
the regions have come up with of how they

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:19.169
might be able to make those changes.

00:04:19.169 --> 00:04:23.700
Paragraphs 3 and 4 are critically important,
about the principles that we have worked in

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:32.540
on the changes, and the table in paragraph
8 shows broadly what the four regions are

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:37.460
proposing, with significant reductions in
the number of sites, and, inevitably, significant

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:42.350
reductions in the number of staff working
on these services throughout the course of

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:46.710
next year, but you will see, not to the original
timetable of April 2014.

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:51.830
You will notice that London and the South
have one joint proposal.

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:59.180
That is largely because the other costs in
London make it almost impossible to undertake

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:07.100
these services within the capital, because
of the additional market forces.

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:10.130
Proposals
These three proposals are what we want to

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:15.190
go and talk to staff about now, so that we
can refine them, improve them and turn them

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:17.710
into one national proposal, one coherent proposal.

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:22.010
I would point out that there are other very
significant pieces of work running alongside

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:25.030
at the same time.

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:29.290
Some of these services are already outsourced,
so we need to be having the right conversation

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:34.500
with those providers to make sure that they
can come in line with this plan.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:39.640
There are some national things that we need
to get in place, especially round IT, which

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:45.710
will support the delivery of these services
from their new and reduced sites.

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:50.150
There are some aspects of the things that
have been put into the bundle of primary care

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:55.860
support services that actually are not primary
care support services; they have just historically

00:05:55.860 --> 00:06:00.389
been bundled with that, and we need to understand
what those are and make a decision where the

00:06:00.389 --> 00:06:03.100
responsibility for those will lie.

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:08.300
We are, however, committed to maintaining
the timetable we had, which is to bring the

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:21.040
Board a proposal in January for the change
based on what has come out of the work and

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:22.410
the consultation with staff.

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:27.220
Staff consultation
The final section, which I am sure Jo will

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:32.410
want to comment on, talks about how we will
consult with staff.

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:36.690
It is a very difficult process for a number
of staff.

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:40.770
Many of these staff are not necessarily highly
paid workers.

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:46.949
They are unable to move around as easily as
some of the staff already working in NHS England

00:06:46.949 --> 00:06:50.660
who went through this sort of change a year
ago; some of them were perhaps more mobile

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:53.280
if we reduced to fewer sites.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:57.970
So we should not underestimate the impact
that this will have on very many staff, and

00:06:57.970 --> 00:07:03.080
we really, really want to make sure that we
get this consultation right with them and

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:06.110
work with them to make it the best it can
be.

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:11.260
Jo-Anne Wass: I will just say a few words,
if that is all right, because I think Barbara

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:13.419
has covered most of it off.

00:07:13.419 --> 00:07:21.490
One thing I do want to reiterate from what
Barbara said is what a significant change-management

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:24.430
project this is.

00:07:24.430 --> 00:07:29.680
It is in many ways an opportunity for us to
live out our values in relation to our relationship

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:31.610
with our staff.

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:37.040
Just to put the numbers in context, as Barbara
says, there are almost 1,800 staff who work

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:38.699
in these services.

00:07:38.699 --> 00:07:43.020
That is about 30% of our workforce; it is
nearly a third of the staff we employ in these

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:50.320
services and, as Barbara rightly says, they
are generally our lowest-graded, lowest-paid,

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:52.150
least mobile staff.

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:58.160
The way we go through this change process
is going to be very significant, I think.

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:02.690
The scale of the savings means that there
will be fewer jobs at the end of it than there

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:08.470
are now, but the purpose of the consultation,
the legal responsibility we have in the consultation,

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:12.840
is to mitigate redundancy, so to try and keep
the number of compulsory redundancies as low

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:13.840
as possible.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:16.130
That is the principal purpose of the consultation.

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:21.850
For that to happen, the consultation has to
be meaningful in the sense that we need to

00:08:21.850 --> 00:08:25.580
share the plans with the staff, we need to
go into the detail, we need to be prepared

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:32.340
to listen and to change them if staff have
better ideas for ways of saving £40 million.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:40.270
The consultation period needs to run for a
minimum of 45 days under the law, but I think

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:44.670
when we get into this we will probably in
some areas run for more than 45 days.

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:50.920
You will see from table 2 that some of the
regions are not proposing to make staffing

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:55.520
reductions before May, so we are starting
early to try and get full engagement with

00:08:55.520 --> 00:09:00.150
staff and their input into the plans themselves.

00:09:00.150 --> 00:09:04.540
I think we will run the consultation at three
levels.

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:09.370
At national level, we will be talking with
staff-side recognised trade unions about the

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:16.630
way we do this, the policy and the principles
that we apply to the whole change programme.

00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:21.290
At regional level, we will be taking staff
and their representatives through the detail

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:27.500
of the plans, getting their views and giving
them a chance to influence the plans.

00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:32.310
At individual level, we will be having conversations
with every member of staff affected by this

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:37.630
process, about how it affects them and what
they want to do.

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:39.580
That is a major management undertaking.

00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:45.570
There are 1,800 staff; we need to have at
least one meeting, probably more, with all

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:49.070
of them, so it is going to take up quite a
lot of management time.

00:09:49.070 --> 00:09:52.540
I do not propose to say any more at this stage,
if that is okay.

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:57.100
I think the Board is going to have the opportunity
to discuss this again in sub-committees and

00:09:57.100 --> 00:09:58.330
in public in January.

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:01.100
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Jo and
Barbara.

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:05.660
We should not underestimate the sheer scale
of this.

00:10:05.660 --> 00:10:12.110
I think the thing that worries me most is
maintaining a continuity of service and improving

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:18.300
the quality of the service that we provide
to primary care through a process of quite

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:22.260
significant disruption of the jobs of those
involved in it.

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:27.130
Improving service efficiency
We talk in here about resorting to new ways

00:10:27.130 --> 00:10:32.580
of working and to new technologies to carry
through the services, and I am just interested

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:40.330
to know how we can ensure that we use this
as an opportunity in relation to such areas

00:10:40.330 --> 00:10:47.830
as medical records, for example, and transfer
of patients, to advance our own efficiency

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:52.620
as an organisation and to deliver higher quality
to those who are at the receiving end of these

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:53.620
services.

00:10:53.620 --> 00:10:59.910
Independent contractors
Barbara Hakin: Interestingly, I think that

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:06.930
the independent contractors have perhaps been
more digital and more computer systems than

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:12.420
many other parts of the NHS, and a remarkable
amount of this is already computerised.

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:17.810
In fact, one of the significant streams that
we have a dedicated individual helping us

00:11:17.810 --> 00:11:24.760
on will be ensuring that the Exeter system
on which a lot of this is based can be successfully

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:26.930
implemented in the new sites.

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:34.490
I do think there is much further to go, and,
potentially in the longer term, a way of streamlining

00:11:34.490 --> 00:11:36.910
and making it a much better and more efficient
service.

00:11:36.910 --> 00:11:41.880
I think in the first instance, it is really
important that we get through this current

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:46.380
change process and keep everything as safe
as we can, and we will need to work very hard

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:49.910
with stakeholders, because, not surprisingly,
not only are the staff extremely anxious about

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:55.110
this, but also the independent contractors
making sure this service is right for them.

00:11:55.110 --> 00:12:01.019
I do not think this will end there; there
will also be the opportunity to do this in

00:12:01.019 --> 00:12:03.240
a better way as technology improves.

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:15.400
Ed Smith: There is no doubt that we need to
do this and that we need to get to a lower

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:17.480
cost of delivery.

00:12:17.480 --> 00:12:26.860
This is ambitious, the timescale and the size
of this, and I said to David as we were coming

00:12:26.860 --> 00:12:34.170
in, the private sector would only do this
in extremis, and they would have a lot of

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:40.090
resource underpinning it and quite a lot of
oversight to make sure that it happened.

00:12:40.090 --> 00:12:49.260
At the moment, I do not have that level of
detail, and I welcome that it is coming back

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:55.450
in January, but I do just wonder whether perhaps
a little more formalised Task and Finish group

00:12:55.450 --> 00:13:02.690
that are non-Exec -- and I am certainly not
volunteering at this stage -- might be helpful,

00:13:02.690 --> 00:13:05.680
because this has high visibility, it is high
numbers of people.

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:16.870
In the scale of our total cost base, it is
relatively modest, but your point about sustaining

00:13:16.870 --> 00:13:23.740
the service in the new environment is equally
critical, and I think it is probably quite

00:13:23.740 --> 00:13:26.600
a high-risk project.

00:13:26.600 --> 00:13:31.480
Having had one or two of those in our history,
and probably in our histories in other parts

00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:35.029
of organisations, that level of oversight,
I suspect, would be helpful.

00:13:35.029 --> 00:13:39.019
Barbara Hakin: If I might add to that, I think
that would be very helpful.

00:13:39.019 --> 00:13:45.170
Firstly, we have recently very significantly
increased the resource to manage the programme,

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:50.459
but what I also think we need to do is to
make sure we have the right external assurance

00:13:50.459 --> 00:13:54.720
in place, so as well as a Task and Finish
group, we have a gateway review on it.

00:13:54.720 --> 00:14:01.970
If the Board were happy, I would welcome the
opportunity to seek significant external assurance

00:14:01.970 --> 00:14:03.720
for what we are doing.

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:06.640
David Nicholson: I think you are absolutely
right.

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:10.650
I think it would be helpful to have non executive
oversight in some way; it would help us, I

00:14:10.650 --> 00:14:13.130
think, to get through all of this.

00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:23.300
Just two points: one of them is that we are
trying to get down to £1.08, but there are

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:27.470
places in the country that do it at that price
and below that price.

00:14:27.470 --> 00:14:30.170
Barbara Hakin: 60-something pence or below.

00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:31.520
David Nicholson: Yes.

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:35.060
The point I am making, it is possible.

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:38.089
It is possible to do it.

00:14:38.089 --> 00:14:42.540
Secondly, we are going to have a discussion
in December about allocations.

00:14:42.540 --> 00:14:46.910
This will create another £40 million to allocate
for patients.

00:14:46.910 --> 00:14:51.529
It is important to do it; as well as understanding
the risk and all the rest of it, it is an

00:14:51.529 --> 00:14:52.529
important thing for us to do.

00:14:52.529 --> 00:14:57.730
Ed Smith: I was careful to say ambitious,
not heroic or courageous, or brave.

00:14:57.730 --> 00:15:01.630
Malcolm Grant: I think the critical thing
there as far as our staff is concerned is

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:07.480
that this was business left over from the
reforms, and we want to make sure that there

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:14.839
is no disadvantage to staff in the way we
handle this process over the way the other

00:15:14.839 --> 00:15:18.810
staff were handled during the process of the
health reform.

00:15:18.810 --> 00:15:25.480
Can I then ask the Board if they are willing
to endorse the direction of travel of the

00:15:25.480 --> 00:15:34.459
programme, to establish an informal Task and
Finish group and to keep a close eye on resources

00:15:34.459 --> 00:15:46.880
to ensure that the activity is adequately
resourced?

00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:49.980
Use of the company seal
I do not think we need to say anything about

00:15:49.980 --> 00:15:55.829
that unless anyone wants to institute a probing
discussion of it.

00:15:55.829 --> 00:15:59.710
Any Other Business
Date of next meeting

00:15:59.710 --> 00:16:07.080
The only item that I have got is the date
of the next meeting, which is to be on 17th

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:11.160
December and will be held at NHS Southside,
Victoria Street, London.

00:16:11.160 --> 00:16:14.060
Are there any other items of business?

00:16:14.060 --> 00:16:15.060
No.

00:16:15.060 --> 00:16:17.220
In which case, thank you all very much.

00:16:17.220 --> 00:16:21.520
The Board itself will now move into any other
business, but may I thank everybody for their

00:16:21.520 --> 00:16:22.350
attendance this morning.

